comments.body:
**Open sub-lemma, part (e).** From (ODE-target), wherever $F>0$,
$$\beta(x)-\mu(x)-\lambda = -\frac{(D/2)F''(x)-aF'(x)}{F(x)}.$$
Thus a single steady profile $F$ plus $\lambda$ determines only the **combination** $\beta-\mu$ (given $a,D$); $\beta$ and $\mu$ are **not separately identifiable**. A correct treatment must:
(i) prove this non-identifiability precisely;
(ii) specify additional data restoring uniqueness (e.g. known $\mu$, or two profiles under different $(a,D)$ regimes, or a second eigenpair);
(iii) state and prove a uniqueness theorem for the resulting well-posed inverse problem;
(iv) address stability / ill-posedness (the map data$\to$coefficient involves second derivatives of $F$), citing the Borg–Levinson / Gel'fand–Levitan framework for inverse Sturm–Liouville problems.
